JULY-2009- ORNL                                                   2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
Reevaluation of Ni-58 resonance parameters in the energy range    2825 1451
thermal to 812 keV, H. Derrien, L.C. Leal, K. Guber, D. Wiarda,   2825 1451
and G. Arbanas.                                                   2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
     The previous set of resonance parameters was based on the    2825 1451
SAMMY analysis of ORNL neutron transmission, scattering and       2825 1451
capture measurements by C.M Perey et al.(1).The present results   2825 1451
were obtained by adding to the SAMMY experimental data base the   2825 1451
capture cross sections recently measured at ORELA by K.H. Guber   2825 1451
et al.(2) and the GELINA very high resolution transmission        2825 1451
measurements performed by Brusegan et al.(3).  A complete         2825 1451
Resonance Parameter Covariance Matrix(RPCM) is obtained from      2825 1451
the SAMMY analysis of the experimental data base made consistent  2825 1451
by neutron energy scale adjustments, and normalization and        2825 1451
background corrections.  The RPCM contains mainly the             2825 1451
uncertainties and the correlations due to the statistical errors  2825 1451
of the experimental data base.  The systematic uncertainties of   2825 1451
the experimental data, estimated from preliminary SAMMY analyses  2825 1451
of the experimental data base, are taking into account in cross   2825 1451
section covariance files for the total, scattering and capture    2825 1451
cross sections.  Since the capture cross sections are very small  2825 1451
compared to the total cross sections the scattering cross         2825 1451
section covariance file has been taken identical to the total     2825 1451
cross section covariance file. Due to the fact that some          2825 1451
components of the systematic uncertainties are not fully          2825 1451
correlated (background, deviation between the theoritical and     2825 1451
expirimental data in the SAMMY fits etc...) all the non-diagonal  2825 1451
elements of the cross section correlation matrices were settled   2825 1451
at 0.5.                                                           2825 1451
     The thermal cross sections calculated from the resonances    2825 1451
are the following, compared to the last Mughabghab(5) evaluation  2825 1451
and to ENDF/B-VII-0:                                              2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
                    Present         Mughabghab      ENDF/B=VII-0  2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
     Total       29.32+-0.48 b     29.67+-0.50 b      29.4  b     2825 1451
     Capture      4.27+-0.15 b      4.37+-0.10 b       4.62 b     2825 1451
     Scattering  25.05+-0.50 b     25.30+-0.40 b      24.8  b     2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
The value of the capture cross section is a weighted average of   2825 1451
the experimental data.  Adjustment of the thermal capture cross   2825 1451
section could be obtained by small variation of the capture       2825 1451
width of the resonance at -1164 eV.                               2825 1451
     The capture resonance integral calculated in the energy      2825 1451
range 0.5 eV to 800 keV is 2.095 b compared to 2.199 b from       2825 1451
ENDF/B-VII-0 and 2.1 b from the last Mughabghab evaluation.       2825 1451
     The direct capture component at 0.0253 eV was estimated to   2825 1451
be 1.52 b from a Direct-Semi-Direct calculation by G. Arbanas(4). 2825 1451
The resonance parameters contribute only for 2.75 b at 0.0253 eV  2825 1451
The direct capture contribution is included in a file 3           2825 1451
background for the total and capture cross sections in the        2825 1451
energy range thermal to 812 keV.                                  2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
