 **************************************************************** 2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
  ENDF/B-VI MOD 3 Evaluation, September 1997, S. Chiba,           2825 1451
          M.B. Chadwick, P.G. Young (LANL), and A.J. Koning (ECN) 2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
 Los Alamos LA150 Library, produced with FKK/GNASH/GSCAN code     2825 1451
   in cooperation with ECN Petten.                                2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
  This evaluation provides a complete representation of the       2825 1451
nuclear data needed for transport, damage, heating,               2825 1451
radioactivity, and shielding applications over the incident       2825 1451
neutron energy range from 1.0E-11 to 150 MeV.  The discussion     2825 1451
here is divided into the region below and above 20 MeV.           2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
INCIDENT NEUTRON ENERGIES < 20 MeV                                2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
  Below 20 MeV the evaluation is based completely on the ENDF/B-  2825 1451
VI.5 (Rev1,Release 2) evaluation by Larson, C. Perey, Hetrich,    2825 1451
and Fu.                                                           2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
INCIDENT NEUTRON ENERGIES > 20 MeV                                2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
  The ENDF/B-VI Release 2 evaluation extends to 20 MeV and        2825 1451
includes cross sections and energy-angle data for all             2825 1451
significant reactions.  The present evauation utilizes a more     2825 1451
compact composite reaction spectrum representation above 20 MeV   2825 1451
in order to reduce the length of the file. No essential data for  2825 1451
applications is lost with this representation.                    2825 1451
  The evaluation above 20 MeV utilizes MF=6, MT=5 to represent    2825 1451
all reaction data.  Production cross sections and emission        2825 1451
spectra are given for neutrons, protons, deuterons, tritons,      2825 1451
alpha particles, gamma rays, and all residual nuclides produced   2825 1451
(A>5) in the reaction chains. To summarize, the ENDF sections     2825 1451
with non-zero data above En = 20 MeV are:                         2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
  MF=3 MT=  1  Total Cross Section                                2825 1451
       MT=  2  Elastic Scattering Cross Section                   2825 1451
       MT=  3  Nonelastic Cross Section                           2825 1451
       MT=  5  Sum of Binary (n,n') and (n,x) Reactions           2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
  MF=4 MT=  2  Elastic Angular Distributions                      2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
  MF=6 MT=  5  Production Cross Sections and Energy-Angle         2825 1451
               Distributions for Emission Neutrons, Protons,      2825 1451
               Deuterons, Tritons, and Alphas; and Angle-         2825 1451
               Integrated Spectra for Gamma Rays and Residual     2825 1451
               Nuclei That Are Stable Against Particle Emission   2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
  The evaluation is based on nuclear model calculations that      2825 1451
have been benchmarked to experimental data, especially for n +    2825 1451
Ni58 and p + Ni58 reactions [Ch97]. We use the GNASH code system  2825 1451
[Yo92], which utilizes Hauser-Feshbach statistical,               2825 1451
preequilibrium and direct-reaction theories. Spherical optical    2825 1451
model calculations are used to obtain particle transmission       2825 1451
coefficients for the Hauser-Feshbach calculations, as well as     2825 1451
for the elastic neutron angular distributions.                    2825 1451
  Cross sections and spectra for producing individual residual    2825 1451
nuclei are included for reactions. The energy-angle-correlations  2825 1451
for all outgoing particles are based on Kalbach systematics       2825 1451
[Ka88].                                                           2825 1451
  A model was developed to calculate the energy distributions of  2825 1451
all recoil nuclei in the GNASH calculations [Ch96]. The recoil    2825 1451
energy distributions are represented in the laboratory system in  2825 1451
MT=5, MF=6, and are given as isotropic in the lab system. All     2825 1451
other data in MT=5,MF=6 are given in the center-of-mass system.   2825 1451
This method of representation utilizes the LCT=3 option approved  2825 1451
at the November, 1996, CSEWG meeting.                             2825 1451
  Preequilibrium corrections were performed in the course of the  2825 1451
GNASH calculations using the exciton model of Kalbach [Ka77,      2825 1451
Ka85], validated by comparison with calculations using Feshbach,  2825 1451
Kerman, Koonin (FKK) theory [Ch93].  Discrete level data from     2825 1451
nuclear data sheets were matched to continuum level densities     2825 1451
using the formulation of Gilbert and Cameron [Gi65] and pairing   2825 1451
and shell parameters from the Cook [Co67] analysis. Neutron and   2825 1451
charged- particle transmission coefficients were obtained from    2825 1451
the optical potentials, as discussed below.  Gamma-ray            2825 1451
transmission coefficients were calculated using the Kopecky-Uhl   2825 1451
model [Ko90].                                                     2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
SOME Ni-SPECIFIC INFORMATION CONCERNING THE EVAL.                 2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
  The neutron total cross section was evaluated based on the      2825 1451
least-squares method with GMA code system [Po81] taking account of2825 1451
the experimental data [Ci68, Pe73, Sc73, La83, Sm92, Di97].  The  2825 1451
data for natural Ni was used above 20 MeV because there was no    2825 1451
data for Ni-58 at this energy region. The data for natural Ni were2825 1451
transformed to the Ni-58 cross section according to A*(2/3) law.  2825 1451
In the GMA analysis, the systematic error was assumed to be 1 %   2825 1451
for all the data set.  Result of the GMA evaluation was used as   2825 1451
the evaluated total cross section data above 20 MeV.              2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
The evaluated total cross section data (1 to 250 MeV), s-wave     2825 1451
strength function[Mu81], and elastic scattering angular           2825 1451
distribution data [Sm92, Gu85, Tu73, Pe88, Ya79] were used to     2825 1451
obtain the neutron optical potential parameters.  The parameter   2825 1451
estimation was carried out based on Marquart-Bayesian approach    2825 1451
[Sm91], where ECIS95 code [Ra96] was used for the optical model   2825 1451
calculation.  We have employed the energy dependence of the       2825 1451
optical potential similar to Delaroche's work [De89]. The initial 2825 1451
potential parameters were adopted from Koning and Delaroche       2825 1451
[Ko97].  Total of 17 parameters concerning the central potential  2825 1451
were estimated with associated covariance matrix.  Presently      2825 1451
obtained potential was used for the calculation of neutron        2825 1451
transmission coefficients and DWBA cross sections in the entire   2825 1451
energy region above 20 MeV.  Below 20 MeV, the Harper neutron     2825 1451
potential [Ha82] was used.                                        2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
The proton optical potential was also searched for to obtain a    2825 1451
good description of proton-total reaction cross section as        2825 1451
predicted by Wellisch-Axen systematic [We96] above 50 MeV.  The   2825 1451
parameter estimation was carried out by the Marquart-Bayesian     2825 1451
approach similar to the neutron OMP, but trying to seek the best  2825 1451
parameter to reproduce the reaction cross sections compiled by    2825 1451
Carlson [Ca96] and Wellisch values. In this search, the           2825 1451
geometrical parameters were fixed to be same as the neutron       2825 1451
potential.  The present potential gives a good description of the 2825 1451
proton total reaction cross section above 10 MeV to 250 MeV.      2825 1451
However, after some trial and error to reproduce both the elastic 2825 1451
scattering and reaction cross section data, we have employed the  2825 1451
following combination of proton potentials:                       2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
 0 to   5 MeV : Harper potential [Ha82]                           2825 1451
 5 to  50 MeV : Koning and Delaroche [Ko97]                       2825 1451
50 to 260 MeV : Present OMP                                       2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
For deuterons, the Lohr-Haeberli [Lo74] global potential was used;2825 1451
for alpha particles the McFadden-Satchler potential [Mc66] was    2825 1451
used; and for tritons the Beccheti-Greenlees [Be71] potential was 2825 1451
used.  The He-3 channel was ignored.                              2825 1451
                                                                  2825 1451
